From: Shigeaki Asai <y-asai.0816@docomonet.jp>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: h618: orangepi-zero3: Add CPU regulator
 ramp delay

With cpufreq enabled, the Orange Pi Zero3 hangs within a minute of boot:
a CPU core stops responding even to NMIs and the PMIC I2C bus locks up.
Booting with cpufreq.off=1 is stable.

Pinning the CPU at its top OPP (performance governor, 1.416 GHz) is
stable as well, so the OPP voltages themselves are sufficient and the
hang comes from frequency switches.

The AXP313A regulator ops have no set_ramp_delay and the driver declares
no ramp delay for DCDC2, so the regulator core assumes a new CPU voltage
is reached instantly and raises the clock right away. Declare a ramp
delay so the core waits for the voltage to settle before the frequency
goes up. 2500 uV/us is the value the H6 boards use for their CPU rail.

Tested on Orange Pi Zero3 (H618, 4 GiB): with the ondemand governor it
previously hung within a minute of boot; with this change it runs games
for several minutes without issues.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h618-orangepi-zero3.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h618-orangepi-zero3.dts
@@ -97,6 +97,7 @@
 				regulator-min-microvolt = <810000>;
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <1100000>;
 				regulator-name = "vdd-cpu";
+				regulator-ramp-delay = <2500>;
 			};
 
 			reg_dcdc3: dcdc3 {
